  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> be the sum of first <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></math> terms of an arithmetic progression. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> be the sum of first&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></math> terms of the same arithmetic progression. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn></math>, then the sum of the first <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mi>n</mi></math> terms of the arithmetic progression is equal to:<gwmw style=\"display:none;\"></gwmw>"
  },
